{
  // `src/` and `protocol/`, with no platform surface at all: `lib` is ES2024
  // and `types` is empty, exactly as packages/domain does it. **This is the
  // program that enforces the boundary**, and it is the reason the wider one
  // above is allowed to admit the DOM.
  //
  // `protocol/` (#41, #42) is in here rather than beside `web-bluetooth/`
  // because the GATT decoders are shared with the native stacks (#15) — a
  // decoder that could name a browser type would be a decoder #15 had to
  // rewrite. `DataView` is an ECMAScript built-in and survives `lib: ["ES2024"]`
  // with `types: []`, which is the whole reason a payload decoder can be
  // platform-free at all; `BluetoothRemoteGATTCharacteristic` and `navigator`
  // do not, and are compile errors there exactly as they are in `src/`.
  //
  // The whole point of #39 is that the same interfaces are satisfied, unchanged,
  // by Web Bluetooth (#40), by CoreBluetooth and the Android BLE APIs (#15) and
  // by the simulator (#44). An interface that can name a browser type has
  // already chosen one of the three — so in this program `navigator`,
  // `BluetoothDevice` and `BluetoothRemoteGATTCharacteristic` are compile
  // errors rather than review findings, and with `types: []` so are `process`
  // and `Buffer`.
  //
  // ⚠️ **`DataView` is NOT.** This comment used to name "`DataView`'s
  // GATT-shaped uses" alongside them, and that was never true: `DataView` is an
  // ECMAScript built-in and is in `lib: ["ES2024"]`. #41 depends on it being
  // there — a payload decoder that could not name a `DataView` could not be
  // shared with the native stacks. What keeps GATT payload out of `src/` is
  // that directory's own documented rule and review; it is not a compile error
  // and must not be believed to be one.
  //
  // CLAUDE.md section 4d: that closure is conditional on nothing in the program
  // pulling a `/// <reference types="node" />` back in through a `.d.ts`.
  // `vitest.config.ts` is deliberately excluded here and imports nothing, for
  // the reason its own header records at length.
  "extends": "../../tsconfig.base.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "lib": ["ES2024"],
    "types": []
  },
  "include": ["src/**/*.ts", "protocol/**/*.ts"]
}
